        "moveId": "name-it-exactly",
        "chapterTitle": "Name the Feeling",
        "tagline": "Name it to tame it",
        "story": "The old book sits there.\nNo new book.\nTheo turns it face down.\n\nTheo dents an empty comic panel. Dad sits beside him.\n\n\u201cI couldn't get another library book,\u201d Theo says. \u201cBecause I forgot to return this one.\u201d He nudges the book without looking at it.\n\n\u201cIt was a bad day,\u201d Theo says. He draws a tiny thundercloud in the empty panel. Then he adds another bump. Apparently this cloud needs room to expand.\n\n\u201cBad can hold lots of feelings,\u201d Dad says. \u201cWould you like to find a more precise word, one that fits more closely?\u201d Theo rolls the pencil beneath his palm. He doesn't want a word that makes forgetting sound fine.\n\n\u201cThe word doesn't have to excuse anything,\u201d Dad says. \u201cIt just names what you feel.\u201d Theo considers that. \u201cMad,\u201d he says, then frowns. \u201cBut not exactly stomping mad. More like I was ready for something good, and then nothing.\u201d\n\n\u201cAn anticlimax,\u201d Dad says. \u201cA big build-up, then a flat ending.\u201d Theo taps the blank space beneath his cloud. \u201cYes. Except the flat ending is also annoying.\u201d\n\n\u201cCan you give me an example?\u201d Theo asks. Dad rests his hands on the table. \u201cSure. Here's one way to find the word.\u201d\n\n\u201cThink of one small thing that bugged you this week. Say the first word that comes. Probably bad, or mad. Now find a sharper word. Annoyed? Embarrassed? Left out? Let down? Jealous? Nervous? Pick one. If two fit, say both. Say it out loud: 'I felt embarrassed, and a bit annoyed.' Now give it a number from 1 to 10. That's it. The word is a tool, not a grade.\u201d\n\nTheo puts the pencil down. \u201cNot embarrassed, I don't think.\u201d He looks at the book again. \u201cLet down. I really wanted a new one. Disappointed is the word.\u201d\n\n\u201cAnd annoyed?\u201d Dad asks. \u201cMaybe,\u201d Theo says. \u201cAt myself for forgetting.\u201d They each touch a hand to their own chest. Theo tries the words aloud instead of leaving them tangled inside.\n\n\u201cDisappointed and annoyed,\u201d Theo says. \u201cDisappointed is bigger. Seven.\u201d Saying seven feels strange. He nearly changes it to six, then remembers that nobody is marking his answer.\n\nDad touches his chest again. \u201cI feel sad that your afternoon was rough.\u201d Theo rubs a dent in the paper. The book is still the wrong book. His chest still feels tight.\n\n\u201cI said it, but I'm still upset,\u201d Theo says. Dad nods. \u201cNaming feelings sometimes helped adults feel less upset. Those results come from adult studies, so we don't know whether this practice helps children.\u201d\n\nTheo picks up his pencil. \u201cAt least I know which upset it is.\u201d He adds a second, smaller cloud beside the first. The small one looks suspiciously like a lumpy potato.\n\n\u201cThat one needs a name too,\u201d Dad says. \u201cAnnoyed,\u201d Theo tells him. \u201cNot potato.\u201d He gives it one last bump, which does not improve its dignity.\n\nDad looks from one cloud to the other. \u201cWhich word fits best right now?\u201d Theo checks before answering. \u201cStill disappointed. And a bit annoyed.\u201d\n\nHe leaves the rest of the comic panel empty. There isn't a cheerful ending to draw just because he has found the words.\n\nThe old book stays on the table. Theo is still disappointed about the new one he couldn't bring home. When Dad sits back beside him, Theo doesn't have to squeeze all of that into \u201cbad.\u201d",
